          "abstract": "<jats:p>Machine learning models have become widely used across many areas in recent years. However, how well these algorithms perform often depends strongly on the specific dataset being used, which remains a key difficulty. To help address this issue, this paper presents a detailed comparison of eight machine learning models applied to the UCI Adult Income dataset. The goal is to predict a binary outcome-whether an individual's income is above or below given threshold. The models chosen for this binary classification task are logistic regression, linear discriminant analysis, quadratic discriminant analysis, support vector classifier, random forest classifier, gradient boosting classifier, artificial neural network, and deep neural network. All models underwent thorough hyperparameter optimization using random search. Performance was evaluated using six standard binary classification measures: balanced accuracy score, confusion matrix, Matthews correlation coefficient, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve, area under the precision-recall curve, and F-measure. The results indicate that the deep neural network delivered the strongest performance on the adult income dataset.</jats:p>",
          "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.6180378",
